BLAKE2b — cryptographic hash (RFC 7693), unkeyed one-shot subset.

Source:   compact clean-room implementation of RFC 7693 / the public-domain
          reference at https://github.com/BLAKE2/BLAKE2 (blake2b-ref.c).
          Same algorithm, reduced API surface: one-shot, unkeyed, salt/personal
          all-zero — exactly the parameters libsodium's crypto_generichash
          uses for the sealed-box nonce.
License:  Public domain / CC0, matching the upstream reference dedication
          (authors of the reference: Samuel Neves, Jean-Philippe Aumasson).

Why vendored here (per the dependency rule, and the vendor/tweetnacl precedent):
  - TweetNaCl's sealed-box helper in src/common/privacy.c derives its nonce
    with SHA-512, which is self-consistent but NOT wire-compatible with
    libsodium's crypto_box_seal (nonce = BLAKE2b-192(eph_pk || recip_pk)).
    The service this talks to speaks libsodium, so the
    cluster-side platform Agent needs a real BLAKE2b for interop.
  - Self-contained: one .c + one .h, C99, no dependencies, builds on
    Windows / Linux / macOS with plain cc -- the same smallest-package philosophy as
    vendor/tweetnacl.

Verified against libsodium: a cross-language end-to-end test seals with real
libsodium-wrappers and opens in C (and vice versa); a wrong nonce would fail
the Poly1305 MAC, so a green e2e is a proof of BLAKE2b correctness for the
64-byte unkeyed 24-byte-digest case we use.
